# Shape of a call

```zig
const r = try client.ask(state, .{
    .field_name = tai.noul(...) | tai.choice(...) | tai.score(...),
    ...
}, .{});

r.answers.field_name   // typed by the question
r.usage                // input_tokens, output_tokens
r.model()              // e.g. "jev-1.13.0"
```

Each field name becomes the question id sent to the API. Because field names
are unique by construction, you can't send duplicate ids.

# `tai.noul`: yes/no

```zig
.is_urgent = tai.noul("Does this convey urgency?"),
```

The answer is an `f64` from 0 (no) to 1 (yes):

```zig
if (r.answers.is_urgent > 0.8) escalate();
```

Use `.criteria(yes, no)` to say what each end of the scale means:

```zig
.is_urgent = tai.noul("Does this convey urgency?")
    .criteria("Explicitly time-sensitive", "No urgency expressed"),
```

# `tai.choice`: pick one option

Pass a struct literal that maps each option to its description. Use `null`
for an option that needs no description.

```zig
.department = tai.choice("Which team should handle this?", .{
    .billing = "Payments, invoicing, refunds",
    .technical = "Bugs, outages, integrations",
    .sales = null,
}),
```

`tai` generates an `enum { billing, technical, sales }` from the field names.
The answer is a `tai.ChoiceResult` of that enum:

| Field / method | Type | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `choice` | `enum` | The highest-probability option |
| `probabilities` | `std.EnumArray(E, f64)` | Every option's probability |
| `confidence` | `f64` | How certain the model is ([Confidence](https://docs.typesafe.ai/confidence)) |
| `probability(option)` | `f64` | Shorthand for `probabilities.get(option)` |

```zig
switch (r.answers.department.choice) {
    .billing => ...,
    .technical => ...,
    .sales => ...,
}
const p_sales = r.answers.department.probability(.sales);
```

If you already have an enum, pass the type itself. Each option is sent with a
`null` description.

```zig
const Severity = enum { low, medium, high, critical };

.severity = tai.choice("How severe is this?", Severity),
// r.answers.severity.choice is a Severity
```

A choice must have between 1 and 255 options, and the compiler enforces that.

# `tai.score`: rate against levels

Pass a tuple of ordered level descriptions (2 to 10 levels):

```zig
.frustration = tai.score("How frustrated is the customer?", .{ "Calm", "Frustrated", "Very angry" }),
```

or name the levels with a struct literal, so the answer is an enum:

```zig
.frustration = tai.score("How frustrated is the customer?", .{
    .calm = "Calm",
    .frustrated = "Frustrated",
    .angry = "Very angry",
}),
```

The answer is a `tai.ScoreResult`:

| Field / method | Type | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `score` | `f64` | Probability-weighted level; can land between levels (e.g. `1.05`) |
| `probabilities` | `[N]f64` | Probability of each level, by index |
| `confidence` | `f64` | How certain the model is |
| `level()` | `usize` or `enum` | The single most probable level |
| `probability(level)` | `f64` | Probability of one level |

```zig
if (r.answers.frustration.level() == .angry) offerRefund();
```

# State and instructions

`state`, instructions and descriptions accept any value that `std.json` can
serialize, so you can pass records straight in. Refer to fields by name in
backticks:

```zig
const r = try client.ask(.{
    .customer = .{ .plan = "pro", .months = 18 },
    .message = message,
}, .{
    .churn_risk = tai.noul("Is `customer` likely to cancel based on `message`?"),
    .duplicate = tai.noul(.{
        .question = "Is `message` about the same issue as `previous`?",
        .previous = previous_ticket,
    }),
}, .{});
```

A plain string is sent as text. Everything else is serialized as JSON. Values
can be comptime or runtime; only the *shape* (option names, number of levels)
has to be known at compile time.

# Reusing a set of questions

A question set is an ordinary value, so you can declare it once and ask it about
many states:

```zig
const triage = .{
    .is_urgent = tai.noul("Does this convey urgency?"),
    .department = tai.choice("Which team?", .{ .billing = null, .technical = null }),
};

for (tickets) |t| {
    const r = try client.ask(t.body, triage, .{});
    ...
}
```

Name the result type with `tai.Result(@TypeOf(triage))`, or the answers alone
with `tai.Answers(@TypeOf(triage))`.

# Per-call options

The last argument to `ask` is a `tai.Client.CallOptions`:

```zig
const r = try client.ask(state, questions, .{
    .model = "jev-1.13.0",          // pin a version instead of the alias
    .retry = .none,                 // or a custom tai.RetryPolicy
    .diagnostics = &diag,           // see Client & errors
});
```

Pin a versioned model if you've tuned confidence thresholds against it: the
`jev-latest` alias moves when a new release ships.
